select '00000000-0000-4000-a000-000000000002', 'Vegans Explore Travel and Global Tour', replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(t.parameters->>'html_shell', $vt${{TITLE}}$vt$, $vt$Vegans Explore Travel and Global Tour$vt$), $vt${{TYPE_LABEL}}$vt$, $vt$Playbook$vt$), $vt${{BRAND}}$vt$, $vt$Vegans Explore$vt$), $vt${{DATE}}$vt$, $vt$November 11, 2026$vt$), $vt${{PREPARED_BY}}$vt$, $vt$V, with Logan Russell$vt$), $vt${{STAGE}}$vt$, $vt$Locked; Logan queue under way, drafts with Sean$vt$), $vt${{STATUS_SUMMARY}}$vt$, $vt$Locked 2026-11-11 (Sean: &quot;Go with your recommendations, lock it into the playbook&quot;). Travel is listed on merit with a direct-first booking order and labelled paid links; Global Tour 2.0 starts with one co-branded Europe pilot in September 2027, operated by a licensed partner who holds all traveler money. Done since: the Book card&#39;s booking order and labels, the Travel report, the three drafts. Waiting on Sean: approve the drafts; Booking.com approval, then one Beck Hall link.$vt$), $vt${{OVERVIEW_SECTIONS_HTML}}$vt$, $vt$  <h2>What we are building</h2>
  <div class="section-block">
    <p>Vegans Explore becomes the trusted source for Vegan travel: stays, retreats and cruises people can trust, travel businesses that want a relationship with us, and tourism bureaus that see something worth funding. <strong>Global Tour 2.0</strong> is the engine: group tours sold ahead of time, operated by a licensed partner, that fill our Travel listings with real stories and give bureaus a reason to invest.</p>
  </div>
  <h2>The standard (locked)</h2>
  <div class="section-block">
    <p><strong>Listed on merit. Booked the way that is best for the place. Every paid link labelled. Data for destinations.</strong></p>
    <ul><li>Selection cannot be bought (the Michelin rule): a written rubric, reviewers who never see sponsor, bureau or hotel contracts. Paid money buys labelled visibility, never a listing or the approved mark.</li><li>Booking order on every Travel listing: (1) the business&#39;s own booking site with our partner code, (2) their own website, (3) Booking.com through Travelpayouts, labelled <strong>Paid link</strong> at the button, only when there is nothing direct.</li><li>Never: paid placement in a list, a site-wide affiliate script, a listing chosen or ranked by what it pays.</li><li><strong>Booking.com links live on our website only</strong> (its terms): never in email, newsletters, social posts, video, the podcast, the app, coupons or paid search. Campaigns link to our listing page; the listing carries the labelled link.</li><li>Funded content says so: &quot;Travel supported by X; X had no say in what we chose&quot;, spoken and on screen at the start.</li></ul>
  </div>
  <h2>Global Tour 2.0: the 2027 pilot</h2>
  <div class="section-block">
    <ul><li><strong>One co-branded Europe departure in September 2027</strong>: one region, one or two Vegan hotel bases, 7 to 9 nights, 12 to 18 guests (minimum 10). Asia moves to 2028, only if Europe sells.</li><li><strong>A licensed tour operator is the seller of record</strong> (first choice World Vegan Travel) and holds every dollar of traveler money in its trust or escrow account, with EU/UK package-travel protection. Never LESARUSS or Vegans Explore.</li><li>About $4,500 to $6,000 a guest sharing; $750 deposit refunded in full if the minimum is missed; monthly payments; balance 90 days out; go or no-go 120 days out.</li><li>Vegans Explore earns 10 to 15% (or $400 to $600) a guest, paid after departure; one free host seat per 12 guests; film rights; named hotel partners.</li><li><strong>A dedicated Vegans Explore host on every departure.</strong> Sean joins some dinners and does not lead the tour; the terms say so. His LESARUSS travel (Korea, Japan, Europe) is on its own budget, never tour money.</li></ul>
  </div>
  <h2>Partner terms the panel set</h2>
  <div class="section-block">
    <ul><li>Hotels: 8 to 10% of room revenue on completed stays booked direct, paid monthly after checkout, tracked by a code in their own engine; the member perk is a welcome drink, breakfast or late checkout, not a rate cut; no fees, no exclusivity; a monthly click report they can check.</li><li>Group blocks are contracted by the operator: net rates with half board, 25% at signing, 50% at 90 days, release at 60 days, up to 20% attrition. <strong>Contracts by January 2027.</strong></li><li>First approach is personal, from someone who has stayed or eaten there, showing the live listing; the directory deal before any tour ask.</li></ul>
  </div>
  <h2>Tourism bureaus</h2>
  <div class="section-block">
    <ul><li>Home first: Visit Lauderdale and Greater Miami CVB. In-kind first (a hosted media trip); any cash a modest content fee for set deliverables.</li><li>Their fiscal years start October 1: pitch FY2028 between February and May 2027 with 90 days of tracked data (clicks by destination, share of visitors from outside the market, email size and opens, podcast downloads) and one business case study.</li><li>European boards in 2028, with the pilot&#39;s report. State precedents exactly (Aruba: HappyCow 2019, most Vegan listings per capita in the Caribbean).</li></ul>
  </div>
  <h2>The film</h2>
  <div class="section-block">
    <ul><li>One city shot deeply as a 22 to 26 minute pilot plus a sizzle reel, built on two or three local Vegan founders; releases, music and brand placements in writing; YouTube first; the documentary pitched once the pilot shows an audience.</li></ul>
  </div>
  <h2>Never, and not now</h2>
  <div class="section-block">
    <ul><li>&quot;Tax-Deductible Sponsorship&quot; comes out of every deck and page; any sponsor who received it is told in writing (the nonprofit was never a 501(c)(3) and is being dissolved).</li><li>&quot;Book with us&quot; as our own agency: later, through a host agency, after counsel. A Vegan-friendly stay standard modeled on the Pre-Vegan Certification: not promised until built.</li></ul>
  </div>
